WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.542
I thought I was going to be a 
psychology major in college

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:15.148
and then I realized I was making all of 
my papers about the banjo anyway.

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:17.917
My name is Jake Blount.

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:21.621
I am a performer and scholar 
of traditional Black folk music.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.323
I live in Providence, Rhode Island.

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:25.158
I live in Providence, Rhode Island.

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:34.067
What's the name of it?

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.135
Altamont.

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:36.302
Altamont. Got it.

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.271
I first heard that song on 

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:41.341
the field recording from 
Murph Gribble, John Lusk and Albert York

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:44.077
which is on a release from 
the Library of Congress

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.847
called Black Stringband Music from 
the Library of Congress.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:50.517
Say what you will about the title.

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:52.118
It's easy to find, you know.

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:57.657
There's Albert Yorke playing guitar

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:58.825
John Lusk playing the fiddle

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.360
and Murph Gribble playing the banjo.

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:01.728
They were a black stringband

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:04.531
they recorded in the 1940s
and by most definitions

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:06.766
they're actually an early bluegrass band.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.835
But for a variety of reasons,

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:12.205
I would consider their race to be 
probably the main reason

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:15.442
they aren't really embraced as 
part of the bluegrass story.

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:19.112
When I study modern day Black banjo players...

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:22.248
I am studying not only the music 
that they make

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:26.853
but what makes that music significant to them
within its social context.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.157
The banjo was descended from 
West African instruments

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:35.195
and during enslavement and during 
the time immediately following

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:38.331
people associated the banjo 
with Black people.

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:40.133
Toward the end of enslavement

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:44.137
we start to see blackface minstrels
come to the fore.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:46.673
Blackface minstrelsy was this really...

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.210
gross performance practice in which 
white musicians

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.378
would black up their face and 

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:55.682
lampoon Black musical traditions 
for a white audience.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:59.853
That had the effect of 
popularizing the banjo

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:01.888
which was featured very prominently.

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:05.959
And eventually bluegrass emerged.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.928
And that became the main way
that people knew the banjo

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:10.330
and interacted with it.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.031
Early on in the record industry

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.400
when folks started going down 
to the south

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.270
they began to record artists

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.473
and consider how they could 
sell those records.

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:23.743
They decided to confine Black musicians 

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.912
to a category called "race records"

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:29.749
and confine white musicians to a category 
called "hillbilly records".

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:32.652
The end result of that was that Black people

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.954
who played what might have been 
considered hillbilly music

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:37.991
which would have been early country 
string band music...

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:39.759
they were not recorded

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.961
because they weren't marketable 
in that category.

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:45.932
Race records were by Black people 
and for Black audiences

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.868
but were supposed to be blues or jazz.

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.771
Many of the early country musicians

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:54.541
had Black mentors and Black collaborators.

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:57.277
Lesley Riddle, for example, 
who taught the Carter family.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:01.614
Many of the Black musicians who were 
teaching early white country musicians

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.583
did not get recorded themselves.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:06.352
And that's just one iteration of this pattern

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:09.322
we see where the music industry

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:12.192
extracts artwork from Black communities

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:14.994
uses them to generate a ton of money

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:17.730
and then directs absolutely 
none of that money back

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.533
into the community that invented 
the music it's selling.

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:27.907
People spend a lot of time deriding 
and belittling Black folk music traditions

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.042
instead of studying them.

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.244
And that's one of the big flaws

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:34.547
with our current canon of 
folkloric recordings.

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:38.384
But the vast majority of these recordings 
were made by white people.

00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:40.153
We know that many of the Black

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:43.022
string bands at that time, including 
Gribble, Lusk, and York,

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:47.594
had separate repertoire that they played for Black people
then they played for white people.

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:51.197
Which means that probably much 
of this music never got

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:55.101
put down on any sort of 
semi-permanent medium

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:58.972
because it wasn't safe to perform it for them 
in that time and place.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:03.343
My new album, The New Faith, 
is an Afro-futurist story

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:06.713
that explores what traditional 
Black folk music might sound like

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:07.947
post-climate crisis.

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:11.618
My idea for this album was to make 
a field recording from the future.

00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:15.355
It lets me go backward and forward 
in time, at the same time.

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:17.523
Once there were no sun.

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:20.193
Once there were no sun.

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:22.528
And I learned "Once there was no sun" 

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:24.230
from a recording of Bessie Jones.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.933
Once there was no sun.

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:31.204
Lord, once there was no sun.

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:34.440
She was a fantastic singer

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.277
and lived for most of her life

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:39.579
in Georgia amongst the 
Gullah Geechee people.

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:44.517
It describes the world before the 
creation of Sun and Moon.

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:48.221
This particular song comes out of 
a ring shout tradition.

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.793
The ring shout is a religious ritual

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:57.063
that's been passed down among the Gullah
Geechee people for a very long time

00:04:57.063 --> 00:05:00.066
and it includes someone with a broomstick 
banging out this 

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:03.136
bum bum bum, bum bum bum rhythm.

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:05.772
We wound up just putting that 
on the kick drum

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:07.540
because I tried to come up with 
a different part

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.909
and it just turned into that anyway.

00:05:10.677 --> 00:05:12.812
Once there was no sun.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.648
once there were no sun.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:18.985
Turns out the thing that they've been doing 
for several hundred years

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:20.520
is... right.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.456
I knew I wanted to draw off of this Hans Sloane text

00:05:23.456 --> 00:05:25.692
because it's the oldest stuff that I've studied.

00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:30.063
Hans Sloane made a trip through 
the Caribbean in the late 1600s.

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:33.800
His work is one of the earliest 
written descriptions

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.768
of a banjo that we think we have.

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:38.037
And he included sheet music

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:40.440
for some of the songs that 
he heard people singing.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.743
I was really drawn to this very simple

00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:47.647
but very epic sounding song called Angola

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:53.386
which is just nice call and response arrangement 
between an instrument and a voice.

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:06.666
So the little banjo piece that 
I incorporated there is...

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:12.238
Because of the way the progression 
of the song goes

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:13.773
I wound up changing it to...

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:25.585
A lot of these songs either have 
no hook or only a hook.

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:28.654
There's there's never a verse and a chorus.

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:32.191
And that left me with with 
a little bit of a heavy lift

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.927
as far as finding things to put 
in between the verses.

00:06:35.261 --> 00:06:38.264
And I wound up coming up with these 

00:06:38.264 --> 00:06:39.966
rhythmic fiddle parts.

00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:46.239
The clapping pattern. 

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:48.541
Clapping is very prominent

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:52.145
in all these old ensemble recordings 
of Black folk music.

00:06:52.145 --> 00:06:54.147
It's just, you know, one of the most accessible

00:06:54.147 --> 00:06:55.615
percussion instruments you have.

00:06:55.615 --> 00:06:58.184
When drums are illegal, as they were 
for black people

00:06:58.184 --> 00:07:00.720
for most of this music's developmental period.

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:02.622
I knew that it had to be in there.

00:07:02.622 --> 00:07:05.024
There was no obvious place 
to put it on this

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:06.859
because of the way 
the beat lined up.

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:09.061
And it's a very weird pattern,

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:18.304
even though that's not a sound

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:21.040
or a texture that you would find 
in those field recordings.

00:07:21.374 --> 00:07:24.310
I tried to use the same approach 
of the field recordings

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.846
in building those sounds and...

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.049
I think it wound up integrating pretty well.

00:07:30.383 --> 00:07:32.385
Once there was no sun.

00:07:33.019 --> 00:07:35.721
Once there was no sun.

00:07:35.721 --> 00:07:37.857
Once there was no sun.

00:07:37.857 --> 00:07:40.993
I heard the angels singing.

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:07.620
I think part of what drove me
to this new project

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.923
is thinking about the continuity 
of those sounds.

00:08:10.923 --> 00:08:13.025
Instead of trying to reenact something.

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:15.695
Maybe envision something instead.

00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:18.764
When it comes to the older stuff...

00:08:19.232 --> 00:08:22.201
because those folks were exploited 
by the record industry

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:24.637
because they were misrepresented by academics

00:08:24.637 --> 00:08:27.807
and by PR reps and everybody else

00:08:27.807 --> 00:08:29.675
along the way who was sold this music

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:31.511
but was not part of that community...

00:08:31.911 --> 00:08:35.248
I feel duty bound to set the record straight.

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:36.949
I think there's something that is very cool

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:38.784
about representing those old traditions

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:41.587
and feeling like I'm part of 
a solid lineage that way.

